  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 230 AM CDT Sat Sep 12 2026\n\nOngoing scattered showers and thunderstorms north of the I-64\ncorridor will lift north of the area as a warm front also\ndeparts the area to the north. The rest of the day will be dry\nfor the great majority of the area, but there is a small\n(10-20%) chance of a shower or thunderstorm in NW Kentucky and\nthe KY Pennyrile. Temperatures will again be very warm by\nSeptember standards, with highs reaching the upper 80s to lower\n90s from east to west, respectively. Sunday will see more of the\nsame, but an approaching cold front will try to approach the \nregion from the NW. This may kick off more isolated storms \n(10-20% chance) north of the I-64 corridor Sunday afternoon, but\nagain the great majority of the area will be dry and very warm \nagain. Temperatures will climb into the lower to middle 90s. \nDriven by dew point temperatures in the lower to middle 70s, \nheat index values will climb into the upper 90s to lower 100s. \n\nBy early next week, the frontal boundary will retreat to the\nnorth as a 597 dm H5 ridge expands over the Mid-South. This will\nbring yet another late-season heat wave Tuesday through\nThursday. Driven by 850mb temperatures of 20-22C (in the 99th\npercentile), high temperatures will approach record highs, in \nthe middle to upper 90s, with heat index values in the lower \n100s. Overnight lows will be very warm as well for this time of \nyear, in the lower to middle 70s thanks to continued very \nelevated dew point temperatures in the upper 60s to lower 70s. \nForecast Heat Risk levels are currently forecast to be in the \nmoderate to major range, but as we progress later into the fall \nseason, this heat and humidity becomes more and more anomalous,\nelevating our concern for heat impacts. Therefore, more heat \nheadlines may be required for the middle part of next week. \n\nBy Friday into next weekend, temperatures will cool down\nslightly as the H5 ridge breaks down and a frontal boundary\napproaches the region from the NW. This will bring slightly\ncooler (but still very warm) temperatures Friday into next\nweekend. Additionally, low-end PoPs (20-40%) will return of the\nforecast as well Friday into next Saturday.",
